  "notes": "The archetype of the producer-as-author studio project, modelled in the 1994-1997 Eurodance window only — his 1970s disco and 1988-90 pop eras fail Gate 2 and are excluded, and the volume issues a correction worth repeating: he has **no documented connection to Real McCoy**, whose board is Wind and Hassas. La Bouche is in ARTISTS; this model is the room, and the room covers three acts rather than one, which is where the differences sit. The casting is the identifying parameter: he builds vocal groups \"in which the publicised members merely lip-synced to songs sung by himself and/or session performers\", and he sings on his own productions — so the countermelody is set an octave below the lead, a male voice under a cast female one, rather than an octave above. Because he \"casts genuine gospel/R&B singers rather than hook shouters\", the vamps carry \"R&B extensions — 7ths and 9ths rather than bare triads\" with a relative-major or plagal lift, and the bass is explicitly \"**more melodic than the German Eurodance norm**\", walking between chord tones at a 0.44 root probability. The kit shows the same trace: \"a lightly swung 16th hat and ghosted percussion rather than a rigid grid\" `[LOW-CONF]`, repeated here, encoded as a 0.54 swing with the ghost probability at 0.36. The perc bed carries congas and bongos because the same model was extended in these years to a Latin-pop act and a 1997 Brazilian-crossover production. The arrangement keeps his one structural oddity: \"a genuine vocal-showcase breakdown to piano and lead voice, unusual in this lane, where breakdowns are instrumental\". ⚠ The tempo, drum and harmonic placements are Eurodance-convention-derived by the entry's own statement; the velocities and densities are INFERRED.",
